## 4. Mutual TLS in the app
   concept: payments asks for a client certificate from the mesh CA and reads the caller's SPIFFE id from it; an allowlist decides who may charge
   orders                           OK, payments saw caller=spiffe://mesh.local/ns/shop/sa/orders
   no client certificate            UNAVAILABLE: tlsv13 alert certificate required
   rogue CA, claims orders          UNAVAILABLE: Failed to connect
   orders, expired certificate      UNAVAILABLE: Failed to connect
   reports (trusted, not allowed)   PERMISSION_DENIED: spiffe://mesh.local/ns/shop/sa/reports may not call Charge
   the tap saw 10049 bytes; card number not readable; readable strings: payments
   check ok: orders is accepted and payments knows it is orders, from the certificate
   check ok: no certificate, a rogue CA and an expired certificate are all refused during the handshake
   check ok: reports gets through the handshake but the allowlist answers PERMISSION_DENIED
   orders -> a fake payments with a rogue-CA certificate for "payments": UNAVAILABLE: unable to verify the first certificate; the fake received 0 charges
   check ok: orders refuses a payments impostor before sending anything
   the cost: every service loads its key, certificate and CA, asks for and checks the peer's certificate, maps it to an identity and reloads certificates before they expire, in every language the platform uses

